      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a colorful analogy, but I want to make sure I have the physics right for my exam. Can we move away from the dancing metaphors and look at the actual mathematical relationship? For a cylindrical pipe open at both ends, how do the positions of the nodes and antinodes specifically dictate the wavelength of the fundamental frequency?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly, let's delve into the mathematical heart of the matter. In a cylindrical pipe open at both ends, like a flute, the standing wave patterns are determined by the boundary conditions at the ends of the pipe. At these open ends, the air pressure must be at a maximum, which corresponds to pressure antinodes. The fundamental frequency, or the first harmonic, occurs when the length of the pipe, L, is equal to half the wavelength, λ, of the sound wave.\n\nMathematically, this relationship can be expressed as:\n\nL = λ/2\n\nThis means that the wavelength of the fundamental frequency is twice the length of the pipe. The positions of the nodes and antinodes are determined by this wavelength. In the fundamental mode, there will be a pressure antinode at each open end and a pressure node in the middle of the pipe. The distance between a node and an antinode is one-quarter of the wavelength, so the distance from one end of the pipe to the first node is L/4, and from the first node to the first antinode is L/4 as well.\n\nAs you move to higher harmonics, more nodes and antinodes are established along the length of the pipe. For the second",
      "source": "target",
